// Withdrawing 0.8.0 took three hand-edits across seven files: the release node, five
// capability records whose promoted truth was derived from a release that no longer stood,
// and the schema twice because `withdrawn` had to be declared in two places (AS6, AS7).
//
// That is not a marker. The index POINTS at a commit and never mirrors it — the record says
// so at the top of every cut release — so taking the pointer back should cost what moving a
// git branch costs. What makes it expensive is that `promote` writes derived truth into
// other records, and nothing walks it back.

thin-slice "TS.260821.13" {
    slug "withdraw-a-cut"
    title "Take back a cut in one command, including what it wrote elsewhere"
    kind "command"
    realizes "CAP.release-binding"
    from-storm "ES.260819.01"

    command "withdraw"
    actor "a maintainer who cut a version and should not have"
    produces-event "ReleaseWithdrawn" "WithdrawalRefused"
    needs-read-model "what-is-currently-true"

    trigger "a version was cut, and what it froze turned out not to be worth freezing"
    outcome "one command takes the pointer back, unwinds what the cut wrote elsewhere, and leaves the withdrawal visible"

    scenario #"""
        Given a cut release whose promotion has been folded into several capability records
        When it is withdrawn with a reason
        Then the release returns to planned, the promoted truth it produced is recomputed away,
             and the index of what WAS cut is kept — because a version withdrawn and a version
             never cut must not read alike, and a withdrawal that leaves five records asserting
             a release that no longer stands has moved the problem rather than undone it.
        """#

    step "1" does="the state goes to `withdrawn`, and the reason is required. A withdrawal nobody explained is a cut nobody can account for"
    step "2" does="the index moves to `previously-cut`, so what WAS cut survives and re-cutting has something to carry forward"
    step "3" does="promoted truth is recomputed from what is still cut — which for a withdrawn release means removed, in the same operation"
    step "4" does="publish is unblocked, because the freeze belongs to a release that stands"
    step "5" does="re-cutting a withdrawn version carries `previously-cut` forward, so the record holds every cut a version ever had"

    excludes "deleting the release. A version that was cut and taken back is not a version that \
        never existed, and the whole argument for recording refusals applies to a cut the same way"
    excludes "withdrawing a release somebody has installed. Nothing here can know that — the \
        command refuses on a version the config names as published, and beyond that it is the \
        maintainer's judgement, which is where it belongs"
    excludes "unwinding the BIND. Iterations stay bound: what they contributed did not stop being \
        true because the pointer moved, and re-cutting the same version should not need them bound again"

    layer "doctrine"    reaches="a cut is a pointer, and the skill says what taking it back costs and what it leaves behind"
    layer "enforcement" reaches="a withdrawal with no reason is refused; a capability asserting a withdrawn release's truth is refused"
    layer "docs"        reaches="`why is this version not cut any more` is answered by the release itself"

    claim "C1" text="one command withdraws, and the record is coherent afterwards with no hand-edit" \
        settled-by="a test cutting, promoting, withdrawing, then asserting praxis check is clean — which it was not when this was done by hand"
    claim "C2" text="the promoted truth a withdrawn release produced is recomputed away in the same operation" \
        settled-by="a test asserting the capability records carry no shipped entry naming the withdrawn version, and that entries from OTHER cut releases survive"
    claim "C3" text="a withdrawal with no reason is refused" \
        settled-by="a test withdrawing with no `--because`, asserting a refusal — a withdrawal nobody explained is a cut nobody can account for"
    claim "C4" text="what was cut survives the withdrawal" \
        settled-by="a test asserting `previously-cut` carries the commit and the date of the original cut, and that re-cutting keeps it"
    claim "C5" text="the state vocabulary is declared once" \
        settled-by="a rule reporting a kind whose `states=` and whose `state` field's `one-of=` disagree — AS7, which cost an edit that looked like the schema had not been picked up"

    depends-on "TS.260820.09 — there is a cut to take back"

    useful-alone "a cut stops being a one-way door. Today withdrawing one means editing seven files \
        by hand and hoping praxis check agrees afterwards, which is the kind of operation nobody \
        performs twice and everybody eventually performs wrong"

    attacks "S5"

    cut-from "WALK.260822.02/AS6 and AS7"

    principle-note "the index points at a commit and never mirrors it, which is what makes a cut \
        cheap. Promotion breaks that by writing derived truth into records the cut does not own — \
        legitimate under `a derived field is recomputed by a rule`, and the cost lands on whoever \
        moves the pointer back. A derived field is only as cheap as its recomputation"

    proves "a cut can be taken back as easily as it was made"
    does-not-prove "that it should be. Whether a version is worth withdrawing is a judgement, and \
        making the operation cheap makes a bad withdrawal cheap too"
}
